<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>6.5.2: Factoring Trinomials with Leading Coefficients of 1</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01651</md:content-id>
    <md:title>6.5.2: Factoring Trinomials with Leading Coefficients of 1</md:title>
    <md:uuid>36b22259-ba65-405a-a21c-daf84d9c2e0d</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity (15 minutes)</title>
<para>This activity introduces students to factoring a trinomial of the form \(x^2 + bx + c\) by visualizing how to undo
  the FOIL process. Students will try to think of two numbers that are factors of \(c\) and have a sum of \(b\). The
  factors that meet these requirements will be the last terms of the binomial factors of the trinomial.</para>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>Solve the first example as a class. Pause on each question for students to answer. After students identify the
  correct factors, ask what should happen next. When students are able to correctly say they should be placed as the
  last terms of the two binomial factors, click the <emphasis>Solution</emphasis> button to reveal the factored form.</para>
<para>Then allow students to solve the problems individually. As students finish, ask if there are any questions.</para>

<para><emphasis>Digital Modeling Strategy</emphasis> - To model the use of algebra tiles, have students access the algebra tiles in Polypad at <link url="https://polypad.amplify.com/p">https://polypad.amplify.com/p</link>. Review the different types of tiles that students can access from the left side navigation bar.</para>

<list>
  <item>The unit tile is the small yellow tile that has dimensions of 1 unit by 1 unit.</item>
  <item>The linear tile, or “\( x \)” tile, has dimensions of 1 unit by \( x \) units.</item>
  <item>The quadratic tile, or “\( x^2 \)” tile, has dimensions of \( x \) units by \( x \) units.</item>
</list>

<para>Each of the tiles also has an additive inverse tile: \(-1\), \(-x\), and \(-x^2\).</para>

<para>As with the other methods, go step by step through the process using the algebra tiles. Begin by asking students to drag the algebra tiles needed for the trinomial into the work area. Circulate around the room to check which tiles students have selected. Watch that students have correctly pulled the units representing each term, specifically watching for misrepresentation of negative terms.</para>

<para>Next, have students arrange the tiles into a rectangle. Help students to understand the mathematical convention of placing the quadratic tiles along the upper left corner of the rectangle and then building out to the linear terms and the unit terms.</para>

<para>Having the automaticity and fluency to identify factor pairs of the constant term will help students more efficiently experiment with using the factors to configure the rectangle.</para>

<para>As needed, select one of the problems to model using this process. For instance, use the trinomial \( 5p - 6 + p^2 \) as in question 4.</para>

<para><figure><media alt="Example of trinomial modeling with algebra tiles"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/07fde39a1bee0ac09c0d1c8e15d92f90323f8221"/></media></figure></para>

<para>Continually remind students that not only must they create a rectangle, but the length and width factors must also accurately represent the products of the trinomial. It may be helpful to display non-examples, like the image below, and ask students why this is an inaccurate representation of \( 5p - 6 + p^2 \) (or \( 5x - 6 + x^2 \)). Explain that this rectangle “represents” \((x + 3)(x + 2)\) but the unit tiles of \( +3 \) and \( +2 \) do not produce a product of \( -6 \).</para>

<para><figure><media alt="Non-example of trinomial modeling with algebra tiles"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/2e532d0ea69945af1834e2c98b2d28d10d61e2d1"/></media></figure></para>

<para>As you walk through the use of the algebra tiles, share the following helpful hints with students:</para>

<list>
  <item>Place quadratic terms in the upper left corner of the rectangle.</item>
  <item>Place unit terms in the lower right corner of the rectangle.</item>
  <item>Identify the factor pairs of the constant term.</item>
  <item>If additional terms are needed, add sets of zero pairs.</item>
</list>

<para><figure><media alt="Step by step modeling with algebra tiles - Step 1-4"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/da3466c089b069c95dd135f919f07027850379a3"/></media></figure></para>

<para>Be sure to have students determine the binomial factors that represent the length and width of the area model’s rectangle. In our example, this is \((x + 6)(x - 1)\).</para>

<para><figure><media alt="Example of binomial factors representing the length and width"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/1dc7b38dc3ea8e63e6df9a9412c0a5ed5223f95f"/></media></figure></para>



<title>Student Activity </title>
<para>Let’s factor this trinomial.</para>
<para>\(t^2 + 14t + 24\)</para>
<para>Think:
  What are the factors of \(24\)?
  Which of these factors add to \(14\)?</para>
<para>Here is the table we made in the previous activity:</para>
<table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
        Factors of 24
      </entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
        Sum of factors
      </entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        1, 24
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(1 + 24 = 25\)
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        2, 12
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(2 + 12 = 14\)
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        3, 8
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(3 + 8 = 11\)
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        4, 6
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(4 + 6 = 10\)
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<para>Write down your answers. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<para>You can use the following video if you need some help</para>
<div class="os-raise-d-flex-nowrap os-raise-justify-content-center">
  <iframe><video controls="true" crossorigin="anonymous">
      <source src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/4282f9c6585902ab44d89e3fa21ec7e9d5d984cd">
      <track default="true" kind="captions" label="On" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/3f0ef0fc07a68dad49fa2a586a9a02043d8210e6" srclang="en_us">
    </track></source></video></iframe>
</div>

<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis>
  \(2 \cdot 12 = 24\)
  \(2 + 12 = 14\)</para>
<para>So, use \(2\) and \(12\) as the last terms of the binomials.</para>
<para>\((t + 2)(t + 12)\)</para>
<para>Factor each trinomial. If the trinomial cannot be factored, write “prime.”</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(9m + m^2 + 18\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((m + 3)(m + 6)\) </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(a^2 − 11ab + 10b^2\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((a − b)(a − 10b)\) </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(u^2 − 9uv − 12v^2\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> prime </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(
    5p − 6 + p^2\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((p − 1)(p + 6)\) </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(m^2 − 64mn − 65n^2\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((m + n)(m − 65n)\) </para>
<title> Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>Emphasize to students that for “undoing FOIL” to be possible as a way to factor a polynomial, it must be
  a trinomial of the form \(x^2 + bx + c\). If the coefficient of the first term of degree \(2\) is not \(1\), then this
  is not an effective way to factor the polynomial.</para>
<para>When “undoing FOIL” to factor a trinomial, \(x^2 + bx + c\):</para>
<list>
  <item> Think: What are the factors of \(c\)? </item>
  <item> Which of these factors have a sum of \(b\)? </item>
  <item> Use the factors that meet these requirements as the last terms of the binomial factors. </item>
  <item> Multiply the binomials using FOIL to check your answer. The product should be the original trinomial. </item>
</list>
<title>Video: Factoring Trinomials of the Form \(x^2 + bx + c\)</title>
<para>Watch the following video to learn more about how factor trinomials of the form \(x^2 + bx + c\).</para>
<div class="os-raise-d-flex-nowrap os-raise-justify-content-center">
  <iframe><video controls="true" crossorigin="anonymous">
      <source src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b768d8c99b6a4d262b7d77626f7598859a277e53">
      <track default="true" kind="captions" label="On" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/892dadf86a37130b7b56e4477a71799bee89bd87" srclang="en_us">
      https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b768d8c99b6a4d262b7d77626f7598859a277e53
    </track></source></video></iframe>
</div>


<title>6.5.2: Self Check</title>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>After the activity, students will answer the following question to check their
    understanding of the concepts explored in the activity.</emphasis></para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">QUESTION:</para>
<para>Factor: \(y^2 − 16y + 63\).</para>
<table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
        Answers
      </entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
        Feedback
      </entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \((y − 7)(y + 9)\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: These factors do not produce the term \(− 16y\) or the
        term \(+63\). The answer is \((y − 7)(y − 9)\).
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \((y + 7)(y − 9)\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: These factors do not produce the term \(− 16y\) or 
        the term \(+63\). The answer is \((y − 7)(y − 9)\).
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \((y + 7)(y + 9)\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: These factors do not produce the term \(− 16y\). The
        answer is \((y − 7)(y − 9)\).
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \((y − 7)(y − 9)\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        That’s correct! Check yourself: Use FOIL on the factors to verify that they are equivalent to \(y^2
        − 16y + 63\).
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<title>6.5.2: Additional Resources</title>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>The following content is available to students who would like more support based on
    their experience with the self check. Students will not automatically have access to this content, so you may wish
    to share it with those who could benefit from it.</emphasis></para>
<title> How to Factor a Trinomial of the Form \(x^2 + bx + c\)</title>
<para>To figure out how we would factor a trinomial of the form \(x^2+bx+c\), such as \(x^2+5x+6\), and factor it to
  \((x+2)(x+3)\), let’s start with two general binomials of the form \((x+m)\) and \((x+n)\).</para>
<para>\((x+m)(x+n)\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> FOIL to find the product.</para>
<para>  \(x^2+mx+nx+mn\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Factor the GCF from the middle terms.</para>
<para>  \(x^2+(m+n)x+mn\)</para>
<para> Our trinomial is of the form \(x^2+bx+c\).
  <figure><media alt="."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/cc6b9de6e3165344c6329fec488951bee8002e8c"/></media></figure></para>
<para>This tells us that to factor a trinomial of the form \(x^2 + bx + c\), we need two factors \((x + m)\) and \((x +
  n)\) where the two numbers \(m\) and \(n\) multiply to \(c\) and add to \(b\).</para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Example 1</para>
<para>Factor: \(x^2+11x+24\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 - 
  </emphasis>Write the factors as two binomials with the first terms \(x\).</para>
<para>  Write two sets of parentheses and put \(x\) as the first term.
  \(x^2 + 11x + 24\)
  \((x \;\;\;\; )(x \;\;\;\; )\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Find two numbers, \(m\) and \(n\), that</para>
<list>
  <item> multiply to \(c\): \(m \cdot n = c\) </item>
  <item> add to \(b\): \(m + n = b\) </item>
</list>
<para>Find two numbers that multiply to 24 and add to 11.</para>
<table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
        Factors of 24
      </entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
        Sum of factors
      </entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(1, 24\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(1 + 24 = 25\)
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(2, 12\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(2 + 12 = 14\)
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(3, 8\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(3 + 8 = 11 \checkmark\)
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(4, 6\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(4 + 6 = 10\)
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>
<emphasis>
</emphasis>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 - 
  </emphasis>Use \(m\) and \(n\) as the last terms of the factors.</para>
<para>  Use 3 and 8 as the last terms of the binomials.
  \((x + 3)(x + 8)\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4 - 
  </emphasis>Check by multiplying the factors.</para>
<para>  \((x + 3)(x + 8)\)
  \(x^2 + 8x + 3x + 24\)
  \(x^2 + 11x + 24&amp;checkmark;\)</para>
<para>Let’s summarize the steps we used to find the factors.</para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">How to factor trinomials of the form \(x^2 + bx + c\):</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Write the factors as two binomials with first terms x.</para>
<para>  \(x^2\;+\;bx\;+\;c\\\;(x\;\;\;\;\;)(x\;\;\;\;\;)\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis><emphasis> </emphasis> Find two numbers, \(m\) and \(n\), that</para>
<list>
  <item> multiply to \(c\): \(m \cdot n = c\) </item>
  <item> add to \(b\): \(m + n = b\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Use \(m\) and \(n\) as the last terms of the factors.</para>
<para>  \((x + m)(x + n)\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Check by multiplying the factors.</para>
<para>In the first example, all terms in the trinomial were positive. What happens when there are negative terms? Well, it
  depends which term is negative. Let’s look first at trinomials with only the middle term negative.</para>
<para>How do you get a <emphasis>positive product</emphasis> and a <emphasis>negative sum</emphasis>? We use two negative numbers.</para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Example 2</para>
<para>Factor: \(y^2 − 11y + 28\).</para>
<para>Again, with the positive last term, \(28\), and the negative middle term, \(−11y\), we need two negative
  factors. Find two numbers that multiply to \(28\) and add to \(−11\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Write the factors as two binomials with first terms \(y\).</para>
<para>  \((y \;\;\;\; )(y \;\;\;\; )\) </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Find two numbers that multiply to 28 and add to –11. </para>
<table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
        Factors of 28
      </entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
        Sum of factors
      </entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(−1,−28\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(−1+(−28)= −29\)
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(−2,−14\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(−2+(−14)= −16\)
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(−4,−7\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(−4+(−7)= −11 \checkmark\)
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<para><emphasis>Step 3 - 
  </emphasis>Use –4 and –7 as the last two terms of the binomials.</para>
<para>  \((y−4)(y−7)\) </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4 - 
  </emphasis>Check.</para>
<para>  \(\begin{array}{l}(y-4)(y-7)\\y^2-7y-4y+28\\y^2-11y+28\checkmark\end{array}\)</para>
<para>Now, what if the last term in the trinomial is negative? Think about FOIL. The last term is the product of the last
  terms in the two binomials. A negative product results from multiplying two numbers with opposite signs. You have to
  be very careful when you choose factors to make sure you get the correct sign for the middle term, too. How do you get
  a negative product and a positive sum? We use one positive and one negative number. When we factor trinomials, we must
  have the terms written in descending order—in order from highest degree to lowest degree.</para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Example 3</para>
<para>Factor: \(2x + x^2 − 48\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Put the terms in decreasing degree order.</para>
<para>  \(x^2 + 2x − 48\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 - 
  </emphasis>Write the factors as two binomials with first terms \(x\).</para>
<para>  \((x \;\;\;\; )(x \;\;\;\; )\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Find two numbers that multiply to −48 and add to 2. </para>

<table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
        Factors of –48
      </entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
        Sum of factors
      </entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(−1,48\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(−1+48=47\)
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(−2,24\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(−2+24=22\)
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(−3,16\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(−3+16=13\)
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(−4,12\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(−4+12=8\)
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(−6,8\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(−6+8=2^ \ast\)
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<para>Since the middle term of the trinomial is positive, the sum of the factors must also be positive. This means that we
  are only looking at the factors in which the larger number is positive. This is why factors such as 1 and −48
  are not included in the table.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4 - 
  </emphasis>Use –6 and 8 as the last terms of the binomials.</para>
<para>  \((x - 6)(x + 8)\) </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 5 - 
  </emphasis>Check.</para>
<para>  \(\begin{array}{l}(x -
  6)(x+8)\\x^2\;–\;6x\;+\;8x\;–\;48\\x^2\;+\;2x\;–\;48\;{\style{color:red}\checkmark}\end{array}\)</para>
<para>Sometimes you’ll need to factor trinomials of the form \(x^2 + bxy + cy^2\) with two variables, such as \(x^2 +
  12xy + 36y^2\). The first term, \(x^2\), is the product of the first terms of the binomial factors, \(x \cdot x\). The
  \(y^2\) in the last term means that the second terms of the binomial factors must each contain \(y\). To get the
  coefficients \(b\) and \(c\), you use the same process summarized above.</para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Example 4</para>
<para>Factor: \(r^2 − 8rs − 9s^2\).</para>
<para>We need \(r\) in the first term of each binomial and \(s\) in the second term. The last term of the trinomial is
  negative, so the factors must have opposite signs.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 - 
  </emphasis>Note that the first terms are \(r\) and the last terms contain \(s\).</para>
<para>  \((r \;\;\;\; s)(r \;\;\;\; s)\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 - 
  </emphasis>Find the numbers that multiply to −9 and add to −8.</para>
<table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
        Factors of −9
      </entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
        Sum of factors
      </entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(-1,9\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(−1+9=8\)
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(1,-9\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(1+(−9)=−8 \checkmark \)
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(3,−3\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(3+(−3)=0\)
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<para>
  <emphasis>Step 3  -</emphasis> Use 1 and –9 as coefficients of the last terms. </para>
<para>  \((r+s)(r−9s)\) </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4 - 
  </emphasis>Check.</para>
<para>  \(\begin{array}{l}(r-9s)(r+s)\\r^2+rs-9rs-9s^2\\r^2-8rs-9s^2\end{array}\)</para>
<para>It is important to keep in mind that some trinomials are prime. The only way to be certain a trinomial is prime is to
  list all the possibilities and show that none of them work. If there are no factor pairs that fit all the
  requirements, then the trinomial is prime.</para>
<para>Let’s summarize the method we just developed to factor trinomials of the form \(x^2 + bx + c\).</para>

<note>
  <para class="os-raise-text-bold">STRATEGY FOR FACTORING TRINOMIALS OF THE FORM \(x^2+bx+c\)</para>
  <hr/>
  <para>When we factor a trinomial, we look at the signs of its terms first to determine the signs of the binomial factors.
  </para>
  <para>
    <m:math display="block">
      <m:semantics>
        <m:mrow>
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mtable>
              <m:mtr>
                <m:mtd>
                  <m:mtable>
                    <m:mtr>
                      <m:mtd>
                        <m:msup>
                          <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                          <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                        </m:msup>
                        <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                        <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                        <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                        <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                        <m:mi>c</m:mi>
                      </m:mtd>
                    </m:mtr>
                    <m:mtr>
                      <m:mtd>
                        <m:mrow>
                          <m:mo>(</m:mo>
                          <m:mrow>
                            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                            <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                            <m:mi>m</m:mi>
                          </m:mrow>
                          <m:mo>)</m:mo>
                        </m:mrow>
                        <m:mrow>
                          <m:mo>(</m:mo>
                          <m:mrow>
                            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                            <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                            <m:mi>n</m:mi>
